H4 cross-project test: pattern does NOT recur; protection is structural

Tested the untested half of H4 -- whether the fabrication pattern found in
pineal-godseye recurs in sibling projects. It does not, and the reason is
structural rather than lucky.

genome-anomaly-lab:
  DATASET_CATALOG.csv carries per-row accession, URL, bytes_expected,
  bytes_actual and sha256. I verified the recorded digests against the actual
  files: 2/2 matched. The important detail is HOW they are produced --
  gal/data/acquire.py computes _sha256(dest) from the downloaded bytes at
  acquisition time (lines 205, 292). The catalog is a RECEIPT GENERATED BY THE
  ACQUISITION PROCESS, not a hand-typed table.

ScientificDiscoveryLab:
  121 Python files reference hashes. The verification is enforced in code:
  experiment_verify.py sets report[artifact_hash_valid] by comparing the actual
  file digest against the recorded one, and test_read_only_audit.py /
  test_t03_formula.py assert recorded digests against freshly computed
  hashlib.sha256() of the file bytes.

THE PATTERN:
  pineal-godseye     hand-transcribed literals in a .py file   -> fabricable
  genome-anomaly-lab digest computed from bytes at download -> not fabricable
  ScientificDiscLab  digest compared in code at verify time  -> not fabricable

The discriminator is not discipline or care. It is WHERE THE NUMBER COMES FROM.
A value that originates as a digest of real bytes has a receipt that cannot be
forged by fitting. A value typed by hand has none, and a validator checking
only internal consistency -- sum-to-one, in-range, plausible -- will accept a
well-fitted fabrication every time.

RECOMMENDATION: where a project must record numeric values that did not come
from a digest or a direct computation, require a per-cell source string and
validate that every cell has one. Now enforced in pineal-godseye's build.
